DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       mmeemmuussaaggee  is  a  bbaasshh(1) script which profiles memory usage of the pro‐
       gram,  _p_r_o_g_r_a_m.   It  preloads  the  lliibbmmeemmuussaaggee..ssoo  library  into  the
       caller’s  environment  (via  the  LLDD__PPRREELLOOAADD  environment variable; see
       lldd..ssoo(8)).  The lliibbmmeemmuussaaggee..ssoo library traces memory allocation by  in‐
       tercepting  calls to mmaalllloocc(3), ccaalllloocc(3), ffrreeee(3), and rreeaalllloocc(3); op‐
       tionally, calls to mmmmaapp(2), mmrreemmaapp(2), and mmuunnmmaapp(2) can also be inter‐
       cepted.

       mmeemmuussaaggee can output the collected data in textual form, or it  can  use
       mmeemmuussaaggeessttaatt(1)  (see  the --pp option,  below) to create a PNG file con‐
       taining graphical representation of the collected data.

   MMeemmoorryy uussaaggee ssuummmmaarryy
       The "Memory usage summary"  line  output  by  mmeemmuussaaggee  contains  three
       fields:

           hheeaapp ttoottaall
                  Sum  of  _s_i_z_e  arguments of all mmaalllloocc(3) calls, products of
                  arguments (_n*_s_i_z_e) of all ccaalllloocc(3) calls, and sum of _l_e_n_g_t_h
                  arguments of all mmmmaapp(2) calls.  In the case  of  rreeaalllloocc(3)
                  and  mmrreemmaapp(2),  if  the new size of an allocation is larger
                  than the previous size, the sum of all such differences (new
                  size minus old size) is added.

           hheeaapp ppeeaakk
                  Maximum of all _s_i_z_e arguments of mmaalllloocc(3), all products  of
                  _n*_s_i_z_e  of  ccaalllloocc(3),  all  _s_i_z_e  arguments  of rreeaalllloocc(3),
                  _l_e_n_g_t_h arguments  of  mmmmaapp(2),  and  _n_e_w___s_i_z_e  arguments  of
                  mmrreemmaapp(2).

           ssttaacckk ppeeaakk
                  Before  the  first call to any monitored function, the stack
                  pointer address (base stack pointer) is saved.   After  each
                  function  call, the actual stack pointer address is read and
                  the difference from the base stack  pointer  computed.   The
                  maximum of these differences is then the stack peak.

       Immediately  following  this  summary  line,  a  table shows the number
       calls, total memory allocated or  deallocated,  and  number  of  failed
       calls for each intercepted function.  For rreeaalllloocc(3) and mmrreemmaapp(2), the
       additional  field "nomove" shows reallocations that changed the address
       of a block, and the additional "dec" field shows reallocations that de‐
       creased the size of the block.  For rreeaalllloocc(3),  the  additional  field
       "free"  shows  reallocations that caused a block to be freed (i.e., the
       reallocated size was 0).

       The "realloc/total memory" of the table output by mmeemmuussaaggee does not re‐
       flect cases where rreeaalllloocc(3) is used to reallocate a block of memory to
       have a smaller size than previously.  This can cause sum of all  "total
       memory" cells (excluding "free") to be larger than the "free/total mem‐
       ory" cell.

